<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congo–Ocean Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chemin de fer Congo-Océan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congo–Ocean Railway runs for about 500 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pointe-noire">Pointe-Noire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>. It was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a> between 1921 and 1934 with forced labour recruited across the federation, including Ubangi-Shari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>. Disease, hunger and brutal conditions killed many thousands of workers, and the scandal was exposed by writers such as André Gide and Albert Londres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congo-ocean-railway.jpg" alt="Congo–Ocean Railway" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jkan997 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Railways_in_Congo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
